    "date": "2026-07-25",
    "trigger": "Independent post-merge review of merged PR #174 (merge commit b8eb90cc10e7593df794f1f79ee96eb2ff50d847), performed per this task's own required workflow step.",
    "findingSummary": "guardAgainstBlsGenericSubstringCollision originally required decision.status === 'accepted' before running. buildResolverDecision always sets best = sorted[0] whenever any candidate exists regardless of computed status, and LogFoodFromRawInputUseCase persists decision.best whenever score >= 0.7 without ever consulting decision.status. So an ordinary ambiguous (MULTIPLE_CLOSE_MATCHES) decision with a high-scoring substring-collision best populated was fully exploitable through production, bypassing the original guard entirely.",
    "empiricallyVerifiedExploitCases": ["Anis", "Mate", "Tee", "Fleisch", "Erdbeere"],
    "fix": "Removed the status==='accepted' restriction; guard now runs whenever decision.best is BLS-sourced with score >= 0.7 (mirroring LogFoodFromRawInputUseCase's own persistence gate exactly), regardless of decision.status.",
    "auditToolingCorrectionsAlsoFound": [
      "classifyMatch's whole_token check originally used record.tokens (includes words from parenthetical grading-code content preserved by alias generation, e.g. '(KA II)' surviving as bare 'ka'/'ii'), disagreeing with production's own normalizedName-based check. Fixed to use normalizedName.",
      "classifyMatch's alias_substring check did not distinguish a whole word within a documented-synonym alias (e.g. 'mezcal' within 'Agavenbrand (Mezcal/Tequila)') from a genuine substring-fragment coincidence, mislabeling 25 legitimate matches as risk. Fixed with a whole-alias-word exemption."
